.TH COLRM 1 "September 20, 2026"
.UC 4
.SH NAME
colrm \- remove columns from standard input
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B colrm
.RI [ start
.RI [ stop ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B colrm
copies standard input to standard output while removing the inclusive column
range selected by
.I start
and
.IR stop .
Column numbering starts at one. If only
.I start
is present, the command removes that column and the remainder of each line.
With neither argument, the command copies its input.
.PP
An ordinary byte, including a null or a byte with the high bit set, advances
one column. A tab advances to the next multiple of eight. A backspace moves
back one column without crossing column zero. A newline resets the count.
.PP
Column arguments contain decimal digits only and must be greater than zero.
The command rejects a reversed range or a column count that cannot be
represented by an unsigned long.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The command exits with status zero after a complete read and final output
flush. Invalid arguments, column overflow, and input or output errors produce
status one.
.SH SEE ALSO
awk(1), cut(1), paste(1)
.SH HISTORY
The colrm command appeared in 3BSD.
